  urldate = {2026-11-23},
  abstract = {(1) The annual maintenance coefficient, 0.815 g g-1 yr-1 (95\% C.L. 0.260-1.231), and the efficiency of food utilization, 2.167 (95\% C.L. 1.193-3.141), were estimated from feeding experiments in flowing-water channels using four adult Esox lucius, size range 624-1010 g. (2) The annual food consumption of E. lucius in the River Frome was estimated to be 151.06 kg ha-1 (95\% C.L. 122.14-207.74). This supported an annual E. lucius production of 41.34 kg ha-1 (95\% C.L. 33.42-56.82). The proportion of food used for maintenance by each age-group increased from 24.3\% at age 1 to 77.5\% at age 10 years. (3) Esox lucius aged 1 year and older were predominantly piscivorous; less than 1\% by weight of their prey were invertebrate animals. Variations in the composition of E. lucius diets resulted from changes in the relative influences of prey availability, food selection by E. lucius, and the seasonal abundance of prey. (4) Young E. lucius ate chiefly Leuciscus leuciscus, Gobio gobio, Phoxinus phoxinus, Noemacheilus barbatulus and, in April, migrating Salmo salar smolts; larger E. lucius ate chiefly L. leuciscus, Salmo trutta, Thymallus thymallus, Anguilla anguilla and small E. lucius. Cannibalism on E. lucius aged between 6 months and 2 years accounted for most of their natural mortality. It is suggested that, in game fisheries, the removal of coarse fish and large E. lucius may not always result in increased numbers of salmonids.},
  file = {/Users/qp263372/Zotero/storage/7X2KEIGQ/Mann - 1982 - The Annual Food Consumption and Prey Preferences of Pike (Esox lucius) in the River Frome, Dorset.pdf}
}

